# Small Business Network Security, Segmentation & High Availability Lab

## Overview

This lab was designed as a small business network environment focused on network segmentation, access control, wireless separation, server infrastructure, security monitoring, and fault tolerance.

The goal was to build a network where different departments and device types are separated using VLANs, while still allowing the traffic each group actually needs.

The environment includes employee systems, management devices, servers, security cameras, VoIP phones, guest Wi-Fi, a DMZ, honeypots, firewall protection, routing, ACLs, and redundant Layer 3 switches and routers for failover.

## Environment

The lab was built using NetworkSim in a browser-based network simulation environment.

The topology includes:

- Layer 3 switching
- VLAN segmentation
- Inter-VLAN routing
- Routers
- Firewall
- Redundant Layer 3 switches
- Redundant company routers
- High-availability failover
- Employee and management wireless networks
- Guest Wi-Fi
- DNS, FTP, HTTP/HTTPS, and NAS services
- Network printer
- Security cameras and NVR
- VoIP phones
- DMZ
- Honeypot systems
- ACL-based traffic filtering
- Deny-by-default access control

## Network Design

The network was divided into separate VLANs based on function and trust level.

| VLAN | Purpose | Network |
|------|---------|---------|
| 10 | Servers | 10.0.1.0/24 |
| 20 | Security | 10.0.2.0/24 |
| 30 | Management | 10.0.3.0/24 |
| 40 | Staff | 10.0.4.0/24 |
| 50 | Voice | 10.0.5.0/24 |
| 60 | Guest | 10.0.6.0/24 |
| 70 | DMZ | 10.0.7.0/24 |

## Topology

The network uses centralized switching and routing with firewall protection at the network edge.

Internal devices are logically separated into different VLANs even though multiple networks share the same switching infrastructure.

The environment also includes redundant Layer 3 switches and redundant company routers to reduce single points of failure.

The Layer 3 switches are configured as a high-availability pair using an active/passive design. One switch handles normal traffic while the second remains available as a standby device for failover.

The redundant routers provide an alternate routing path if the primary company router becomes unavailable.

## Servers and Services

The server VLAN contains multiple internal services:

- FTP server
- DNS server
- HTTP/HTTPS server
- NAS server
- Network printer

The security VLAN contains:

- Security Camera 1
- Security Camera 2
- NVR / Video Surveillance Server

The NVR stores and manages video generated by the IP security cameras.

## Wireless Network

The wireless environment uses separate SSIDs mapped to different VLANs.

The staff wireless access point provides:

- Management SSID mapped to VLAN 30
- Staff SSID mapped to VLAN 40

The guest wireless access point is mapped to VLAN 60.

Guest devices are allowed internet access but are blocked from reaching internal corporate networks.

## DMZ and Honeypot Environment

The DMZ is located in VLAN 70 and contains:

- Honeypot endpoint 1
- Honeypot endpoint 2
- Honeypot server

The honeypot systems are treated as untrusted and intentionally isolated from internal business systems.

ACL rules prevent the DMZ from initiating connections into the server, security, management, staff, and voice VLANs.

This limits lateral movement if a honeypot system becomes compromised.

## Access Control

The network uses a deny-by-default ACL policy.

Traffic that is not explicitly permitted is blocked.

Examples of allowed traffic include:

- Management to approved internal systems
- Staff to approved internal services
- Staff to the internet
- Guest to the internet

Examples of blocked traffic include:

- Staff to Management
- Security devices to Staff
- Guest to Servers
- Guest to Security
- Guest to Management
- Guest to Staff
- Guest to Voice
- Guest to DMZ
- DMZ to Servers
- DMZ to Security
- DMZ to Management
- DMZ to Staff
- DMZ to Voice

## High Availability and Failover

A major part of this lab was reducing single points of failure.

The network includes:

- Two Layer 3 switches
- Two company routers
- High-availability pairing between the Layer 3 switches
- Active/passive failover

The primary Layer 3 switch operates as the active device.

The secondary Layer 3 switch operates as the standby device and can take over if the primary switch becomes unavailable.

The redundant routing design also provides an alternate path if one router fails.

This improves network availability and demonstrates the difference between a network that is only secure and one that is also resilient.

## Security Goals

The main security and availability goals of the lab were:

- Separate device types and user groups using VLANs
- Restrict unnecessary communication between networks
- Protect management systems
- Isolate guest devices
- Isolate security cameras
- Separate voice traffic
- Protect internal systems from the DMZ
- Use deny-by-default access control
- Reduce lateral movement
- Reduce single points of failure
- Provide failover for critical network infrastructure

## What I Learned

This lab helped me understand the difference between physical connectivity, logical segmentation, access control, and high availability.

Multiple devices can share the same switching infrastructure while still belonging to separate logical networks using VLANs.

I also learned that VLANs alone do not fully secure a network. Once routing exists between VLANs, ACLs or firewall rules are still required to control which networks can communicate.

The guest and DMZ networks were especially important because they represent lower-trust environments that should not have unrestricted access to internal business systems.

Another major takeaway was understanding fault tolerance. A secure network can still become unavailable if a critical switch or router fails. Adding redundant Layer 3 switches and routers provides alternate infrastructure so that one hardware failure does not necessarily bring down the entire network.

The active/passive high-availability configuration also demonstrated how a standby Layer 3 switch can take over if the primary device fails.

